  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nCool air in place across the Great Lakes, still reinforced by nw low-\nlevel winds. A sharp shortwave trof has departed to the east, and \nmid-level heights are rebounding, quickly. There was a brief\nmid/late evening uptick in lake effect into the 'big 5'\nnorthwest flow counties. Monitoring snow trends in Presque Isle\nCo as well. But snow intensity/coverage is overall waning again.\nAnd outside of where it is snowing, skies have tended to clear.\n\n1037mb high pressure is near the IA/MN border, and will move east \nacross lower MI this afternoon. Even as it heads eastward, it will \nextend ridging back toward northern MI thru Wed. 1000-850mb \nwinds will start backing to the w/wsw after 12Z, while \nweakening. This is also when the 850mb thermal trof exits east, \nwith 850mb temps climbing to -5/-6C. The increasingly \nanticyclonically curved flow, and more broadly drier air, will \neat away at lake effect precip further. Have of course had to \nincrease pops over guidance into this morning, as models are way\nway too fast to erode lake effect snow. But very small \nadditional snow accums. Relatively quiet Tue night into Wed \nnight, even as systems load up to our sw and s. \n\nSignificant cyclogenesis proceeds over the southeast US beginning \nmidweek. The interaction of systems over the eastern half of the \nCONUS still has associated uncertainty. But a certain amount of \nphasing will eventually occur between a negatively-tilted \nsouthern stream upper trof moving along the Gulf and southern \nAtlantic coasts, and a trailing upper low in the mid-MS/OH \nValleys. The resulting surface low tracks from FL all the way to\nthe far lower lakes by Friday-ish, before departing to the ne. \nIt's an impressive, dynamic system, but a little too far away \nfor major impacts here, at least the way things look now. \nPrecip chances expand into northern lower MI as soon as\nThanksgiving afternoon, continuing into Friday, with the \nhighest pops nearer Saginaw Bay. Most of this looks like rain, \nwith a mix possible at times over the higher terrain of \nnorthern lower. Significant snowfall appears unlikely.\n\nAfter that, some upper trofs will be migrating across southern \nCanada and the northern US next weekend. Precise timing/locations \nare uncertain, but chancy pops will linger in much of northern MI. \n\nTemps start off cool today/Wednesday, then rebound a bit, with \nSaturday for now the mildest day.",
